  <h2>When to apply</h2>
  <p>Timing matters more than students expect. Practices plan their intake months ahead, so if you want a summer placement you should be sending applications in the winter and early spring, not in June. For a full year-out position between Part 1 and Part 2, start the search in the autumn of your final undergraduate year. Applying early does two things: it reaches practices before their budget for interns is spent, and it signals that you are organised - a quality every studio values. If you have missed the ideal window, apply anyway; intern needs shift when projects win planning or staff move on.</p>

  <h2>Where to look</h2>
  <p>Advertised roles are only part of the picture. Check practice websites directly, architecture job boards, your university careers service and RIBA's own listings. But the majority of student placements are never formally advertised - they come from a well-aimed speculative email or a personal connection. Follow practices whose work you admire on social media, note who is winning competitions locally, and pay attention to lecturers and visiting critics, many of whom run studios themselves.</p>

