  <h2>The speculative application</h2>
  <p>Most placements come from speculative applications, so this is worth doing well. A speculative application is simply reaching out to a practice that is not advertising, to ask whether they need help. The mistake students make is sending the same generic message to fifty studios. A far smaller number of genuinely tailored emails will beat a mass mailout every time. Address a named person, not "Dear Sir/Madam". Say specifically why this practice - a project, a material approach, a way of working you admire. Keep it short: a tight paragraph, an attached one or two page portfolio sample, and a link to your full portfolio and CV. Then follow up politely once, a couple of weeks later, if you hear nothing.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Tip.</strong> Send a compact, well-designed portfolio PDF - a few of your strongest pages - rather than a huge file. Busy architects skim on a phone between meetings, and a 40MB attachment that will not open costs you the job before anyone has read a word.</div>

  <h2>What practices actually want</h2>
  <p>Interns are rarely expected to design a building on day one. What practices want is someone reliable, curious and easy to have in the studio. Practical software skills genuinely help: a working knowledge of CAD, a BIM tool such as Revit, and the ability to produce clean drawings and quick physical or digital models makes you immediately useful. Beyond software, the traits that get you kept on and asked back are attitude - turning up, listening, asking sensible questions, taking feedback without sulking and following things through. A modest skill set paired with a great attitude beats brilliance that is hard to work with.</p>

  <h2>Your portfolio and CV</h2>
  <p>Your portfolio is doing most of the talking, so make it easy to read at a glance: strong images, minimal text, and a clear sense of what you did on each project. Tailor which projects you show to each practice - lead with the work closest to what they do. Pair it with a clean one-page CV. If you need help, see our <a href="/career/architecture-cv/">architecture CV tips</a>.</p>

  <h2>Handling rejection</h2>
  <p>You will hear "no", or more often hear nothing at all, and it is not a verdict on your talent - studios simply have limited desks and budgets. Keep a simple list of who you have contacted and when, keep refining your portfolio, and keep sending. Persistence, applied politely and consistently, is what eventually turns into a yes.</p>

  <div class="callout warn"><strong>Routes vary.</strong> Qualification paths, titles and figures differ by country and change over time - always check the current requirements of your national registration body (e.g. ARB/RIBA in the UK, NCARB in the US).</div>

